# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of the deployed output. Astro copies
# `public/` verbatim, so this lands at `dist/_headers` and needs no build step.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY THING THAT CACHES THE SITE'S ASSETS. It used to share the job
# with a `[[headers]]` block in `netlify.toml`; that file and the Netlify project are both
# deleted, so there is no second copy of this rule and no host default behind it. Measured
# on production the day the toml went, one host, one deploy, two answers: `/` returns
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` and `/_astro/icons.*.css` returns the rule below.
# The only difference between them is that one path is named here.
#
# Every filename under `/_astro/` carries a hash of its own contents, so a URL there can
# never mean two things, and a year of `immutable` costs nothing. Without this rule each
# hashed asset spends a render-blocking round trip being told it has not changed —
# measured at 168ms and 175ms, transferSize 300, a 304 carrying no content.
#
# THE HASHING IS THE PRECONDITION, not a detail. `tests/build-output.test.ts` asserts
# it separately, because the day one asset lands here without a hash this rule pins a
# stale file for a year and nothing else would say so.
#
# Cloudflare lower-cases header names on the way out, so the case written here does not
# reach a reader either way.

/_astro/*
  cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# THE HEADERS THE ARTIFACT CARRIES WITH IT, rather than the ones an origin happens to add.
#
# WHY HERE AND NOT IN THE ZONE. `calvindotsg.pages.dev` serves these exact bytes with none of
# the zone's controls attached, so a control configured at the zone protects one of the two
# origins this build is reachable at. A rule in this file travels with the artifact and reaches
# both. That is the same argument the plan for governing the origin makes, applied to headers.
#
# TWO OF THESE THREE ARE ALREADY SENT, AND THAT IS THE POINT rather than a redundancy: measured
# on both origins the day this landed, `referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` comes
# from a Cloudflare Pages default. A default is not a decision — it can change under the site
# without anything here saying so. The VALUE below is deliberately the one already in force, so
# writing it down changes nothing a reader receives and only changes who owns it.
#
# A HEADER NAME MAY APPEAR IN EXACTLY ONE RULE IN THIS FILE, and getting that wrong is silent.
# Read from Cloudflare's own asset server rather than inferred: every rule whose path matches is
# applied, in the order written here, and the FIRST rule to set a name replaces whatever the host
# put there — but a LATER rule setting the same name APPENDS to it. So a second `cache-control`
# anywhere in this file does not override the rule above, it produces
# `public, max-age=31536000, immutable, <the other one>` and pins or unpins assets by accident.
# There is no most-specific-match rule to save you. `tests/build-output.test.ts` holds the
# name-per-rule invariant so this cannot be discovered in production.
#
# NO CONTENT-SECURITY-POLICY, deliberately. The standing decision against one is recorded in
# `plans/README.md`; it wants a measurement nobody has reproduced, and half a policy is worse
# than none. `x-frame-options` is the non-CSP way to say the same thing about framing, and it
# is the only one of the two this file can express.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: `x-content-type-options`, which both origins already send, and
# `strict-transport-security`, which the zone sends and the pages.dev origin does not. Neither
# omission is an oversight — the first would be a fourth line saying what is already said, and
# the second is a transport decision belonging to whoever owns the zone.

/*
  x-frame-options: DENY
  referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  permissions-policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), usb=()
